# Shared state → Context (not prop-drilling)

Use **React Context** when data would otherwise be passed through many intermediate components that do not use it, or when a large props bag is threaded only to reach a deep child.

Still functional React only — **no Redux** (see `global.mdc`). Local coordinated state (≥3 related fields) still uses `useReducer` per `state.mdc`; Context is for **sharing** that state (or values) across a subtree.

```tsx
// ❌ don't — drill through intermediates that ignore the value
function App() {
  const session = useSession();
  return <Layout session={session} />;
}
function Layout({ session }: { session: Session }) {
  return <Sidebar session={session} />;
}
function Sidebar({ session }: { session: Session }) {
  return <UserMenu session={session} />;
}

// ✅ do — provide once; consumers read via hook
const SessionContext = createContext<Session | null>(null);

function useSessionContext() {
  const value = useContext(SessionContext);
  if (value == null) throw new Error('useSessionContext requires SessionProvider');
  return value;
}

function SessionProvider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const session = useSession();
  return <SessionContext.Provider value={session}>{children}</SessionContext.Provider>;
}

function UserMenu() {
  const session = useSessionContext();
  // ...
}
```

## Rules of thumb

- **When**: ≥2–3 levels of pass-through props, **or** a large/growing props object only needed deep in the tree.
- **When not**: one parent → one child, or siblings that can stay prop-driven — prefer props.
- **Prefer a typed context + `useX` hook** that throws if used outside the provider (fail loud).
- **Keep providers close** to the subtree that needs the value; avoid a single god-context for unrelated concerns.
- Context value identity: if the value is an object rebuilt every render and many consumers re-render, split contexts or memoize the value intentionally — do not add `useMemo` by default (see `global.mdc`).
